# Public imports

Authors import **only** paths documented as public on each framework addon
page (summarized in `docs/sphinx/source/public-imports.md`). Deep modules under
`api/components`, `api/runtime`, or `gui` that are **not** listed are private
implementation and may move without a deprecation notice.

Prefer the documented path even when a shorter private import happens to work
today. When a documented public path must move, follow the one-release
deprecation window in `docs/sphinx/source/compatibility.md` — the owning addon
page records the notice before removal.

## Stability surface

Until a first `1.0.0` platform release, **public imports and payload
contracts** are the author-facing stability surface — not every internal
module and not every addon manifest `0.1.0` bump. Do not bump every framework
addon version for unrelated refactors; bump when a documented public import or
payload contract changes incompatibly.

## Public surface by addon (summary)

Exact module paths live on each `docs/sphinx/source/addon-*.md` **Public import
surface** table. Start there before inventing an import.

| Addon | Documented surface (symbols / modules) |
| --- | --- |
| `system` | Base component classes (`BaseModel`, pages, widgets, nav, settings contracts, seeds) |
| `encryption` | Setting secret helpers |
| `settings` | `SettingsStore`, store ergonomics (contracts stay in system) |
| `auth` | Security bases (rights, grants, groups, model rights, capabilities), user settings |
| `audit` | `AuditService`, `AuditEvent`, recorder helpers on system |
| `jobs` | `EventJob` / `ScheduledJob` / `BaseJobQueue` / `JobRegistry` |
| `notifications` | Preference / delivery contracts |
| `cache` | `namespace(...)`, `optional_namespace`, ETag/revision helpers |
| `cdn` | No author import surface; public `/cdn/<path>` fallback |
| `attachments` | `Attachment`, upload service, target policy helpers |
| `mail` | `send_email`, optional bridge (`mail_available`, `optional_send_email*`) |
| `http` | `request_with_retries` |
| `legal` | `BaseLegalCategory` / `BaseLegalSection` / banner + assemble helpers |
| `tags` | `assignment`, `types`, `coerce`, `TAGS_OPTIONS_API_PATH` |
| `comments` | `commentable`, `comments`, `section`, path constants |
| `search` | `register_searchable_model`, `index_record`, `remove_record`, `query` |
| `impexp` | `register_portable_model`, run helpers, path/capability constants |
| `webhooks` | `publish_event` |
| `feature_flags` | `is_enabled`, `percentage_bucket`, `clear_flag_cache` |

Which platform addon to depend on: `framework-platform-capabilities`.

## Capability IDs ≠ package ownership

Some capability and component names still use the `system.*` prefix (for
example `system.settings`, `system.jobs`) even though code and migrations live
under the owning addon. Those IDs are historical platform identifiers for
permissions and clients. They do **not** mean the files still belong under
`framework/addons/system`. Prefer documented capability names in security
components; do not rename them casually.

## Private by default

Treat as private unless documented:

- Test helpers and `api/tests/**`
- Migration modules under `api/migrations/**` (runtime-applied, not author imports)
- GUI implementation files except exported renderer maps
- Internal registries, Flask app factories, and boot-only wiring

